      "## Definition\n\nLet $(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $p,q:\\mathsf X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be measurable densities with $\\int p\\,d\\mu=\\int q\\,d\\mu=1$. They define probability laws $P(B)=\\int_Bp\\,d\\mu$ and $Q(B)=\\int_Bq\\,d\\mu$ ([[def-probability-measure]]). Their **root-L2 Hellinger distance**, with the root-L2 normalization, is\n$$h(P,Q):=\\left(\\int_{\\mathsf X}(\\sqrt p-\\sqrt q)^2\\,d\\mu\\right)^{1/2}.$$\n\nThe dominating measure $\\mu$ is fixed throughout a use of this definition. Changing either density on a $\\mu$-null set does not change the value, since the integrand changes only on a null set. This definition does not assert that separate choices of density versions are jointly measurable in an additional parameter."
    ],
    "lem-iid-bounded-variance-bernstein-tail": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m$ be independent, integrable, centered real random variables on a probability space, with $|Z_i|\\le b$ almost surely for a finite $b>0$. Put $S_m=\\sum_{i=1}^mZ_i$ and $V=\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbb E[Z_i^2]$. For every $t>0$, if $V>0$ then\n$$\\mathbb P(S_m\\ge t)\\le\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{t^2}{2(V+bt/3)}\\right),\\qquad \\mathbb P(S_m\\le-t)\\le\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{t^2}{2(V+bt/3)}\\right).$$\nIf $V=0$, then $S_m=0$ almost surely."
    ],
    "lem-iid-posterior-denominator-lower-bound-from-kl-mass": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ be a measurable parameter space, $\\Pi$ a prior probability measure on it, and $p_\\theta$ a jointly measurable family of finite-valued probability densities relative to one fixed sigma-finite measure $\\mu$. Let $P_0$ have finite-valued density $p_0$ relative to $\\mu$, and let $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be IID with law $P_0$. Assume the Axiom of Choice for the imported directional KL convention ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). The jointly measurable density family is explicit input, so no arbitrary-index selection of versions is used here. In the expression below, the weighted log terms are set to zero on $\\{p_0=0\\}$; on $\\{p_0>0,p_\\theta=0\\}$ the log ratio is $-\\infty$ and its square is $+\\infty$. For $\\epsilon_n>0$, define\n$$K_n:=\\left\\{\\theta:\\mathrm{KL}(P_0\\Vert P_\\theta)\\le\\epsilon_n^2,\\quad \\int\\left[\\log\\frac{p_\\theta}{p_0}\\right]^2p_0\\,d\\mu\\le\\epsilon_n^2\\right\\}.$$\nJoint measurability and Tonelli make $K_n$ measurable, as verified below. If $\\Pi(K_n)\\ge e^{-Cn\\epsilon_n^2}$ for $C>0$ and $\\mathrm{KL}(P_0\\Vert P_\\theta)$ uses the direction in [[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]], then, defining the ratio to be zero on the $P_0$-null set where $p_0=0$, the likelihood denominator\n$$D_n:=\\int_\\Theta\\prod_{i=1}^n\\frac{p_\\theta(X_i)}{p_0(X_i)}\\,\\Pi(d\\theta)$$\nis finite and positive $P_0^n$-almost surely and\n$$P_0^n\\!\\left\\{D_n\\ge e^{-(C+2)n\\epsilon_n^2}\\right\\}\\ge1-\\frac1{n\\epsilon_n^2}.$$"
    ],
    "lem-hellinger-distance-controls-l1-and-total-variation": [
      "## Statement\n\nFor probability densities $p,q$ relative to one fixed measure $\\mu$, define $h$ as in [[def-hellinger-distance-between-density-laws]] and set $d_1(P,Q):=\\int|p-q|\\,d\\mu$. Then $h$ is a metric on the induced probability laws and\n$$h(P,Q)^2\\le d_1(P,Q)=2\\lVert P-Q\\rVert_{\\rm TV},\\qquad d_1(P,Q)\\le2h(P,Q),$$\nwhere library total variation is the event supremum $\\lVert P-Q\\rVert_{\\rm TV}=\\sup_{A\\in\\mathcal A}|P(A)-Q(A)|$."
    ],
    "lem-iid-sieve-entropy-produces-exponential-tests": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space with a fixed sigma-finite measure. A probability density is a finite-valued nonnegative measurable $p$ with $\\int p\\,d\\mu=1$, and its induced law is $P_p=p\\,d\\mu$ as in [[def-measure-with-density]]. Let $p_0$ be a probability density and write $P_0:=p_0\\,d\\mu$. Let $\\mathcal F$ be a family of such densities, write $P_p:=p\\,d\\mu$ for $p\\in\\mathcal F$, and let $\\mathcal F_n\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ be nonempty. Let $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be IID under each law used below. The selected metric $d$ is one of:\n\n- the root-L2 Hellinger distance $h$ of [[def-hellinger-distance-between-density-laws]];\n- the full density distance $d_1(p,q):=\\int|p-q|\\,d\\mu$;\n- the density distance $d_2(p,q):=(\\int|p-q|^2\\,d\\mu)^{1/2}$, provided a fixed $B>0$ bounds $p_0$ and every $p\\in\\mathcal F$ by $B$ almost everywhere.\n\nFor $r>0$, let $D(r,\\mathcal F_n,d)$ be the supremum of the cardinalities of finite subsets of $\\mathcal F_n$ whose distinct points have $d$-distance greater than $r$. Suppose $\\epsilon_n>0$, $\\epsilon_n\\to0$, $n\\epsilon_n^2\\to\\infty$, and\n$$\\log D(\\epsilon_n,\\mathcal F_n,d)\\le n\\epsilon_n^2.$$\nThen for every fixed $M\\ge128$ and all sufficiently large $n$, there is a measurable test $\\phi_n:\\mathsf X^n\\to\\{0,1\\}$ such that, for some $c_d>0$ depending only on the selected metric (and on $B$ in the $d_2$ case),\n$$P_0^n(\\phi_n=1)\\le2e^{-(c_dM^2-1)n\\epsilon_n^2},$$\nand $P_p^n(\\phi_n=0)\\le e^{-c_dM^2n\\epsilon_n^2}$ for every $p\\in\\mathcal F_n$ with $d(p,p_0)>M\\epsilon_n$.\n\nThe entropy metric is the same metric used in the separation and test-error conclusion. The $d_1$ convention is the full integral $\\int|p-q|\\,d\\mu$, which equals twice the library event-supremum total variation distance."
    ],
    "thm-iid-posterior-contraction-by-entropy-and-prior-mass": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sample space with a fixed sigma-finite dominating measure. Let $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ be a measurable parameter space with prior probability measure $\\Pi$, and let $p_\\theta$ be finite-valued probability densities relative to $\\mu$ such that $(\\theta,x)\\mapsto p_\\theta(x)$ is jointly measurable. Let $p_0$ be a true density relative to $\\mu$, and observe $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ IID from $P_0$, the law with density $p_0$. Assume AC for the imported directional KL convention ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). The joint density family is explicit input; no arbitrary-index selection of versions is performed in this theorem.\n\nChoose one metric $d$ for both the entropy and conclusion: the root-L2 Hellinger distance $h$ of [[def-hellinger-distance-between-density-laws]], the full density distance $d_1(p,q)=\\int|p-q|\\,d\\mu$, or the density distance $d_2(p,q)=(\\int|p-q|^2\\,d\\mu)^{1/2}$. In the $d_2$ case assume one finite constant $B$ bounds $p_0$ and every density $p_\\theta$ in the model by $B$ $\\mu$-almost everywhere. For each $n$, let $\\Theta_n\\in\\mathcal T$ be a nonempty measurable parameter sieve and put $\\mathcal F_n:=\\{p_\\theta:\\theta\\in\\Theta_n\\}$. Assume $\\theta\\mapsto d(p_\\theta,p_0)$ is measurable; this follows from the jointly measurable density family by integration of the relevant nonnegative distance integrands. Let $D(r,\\mathcal F_n,d)$ be the packing number defined in [[lem-iid-sieve-entropy-produces-exponential-tests]]. Suppose $C>0$, $\\epsilon_n>0$, $\\epsilon_n\\to0$, $n\\epsilon_n^2\\to\\infty$, and\n$$\\log D(\\epsilon_n,\\mathcal F_n,d)\\le n\\epsilon_n^2,\\qquad \\Pi(\\Theta_n^c)\\le e^{-(C+4)n\\epsilon_n^2},$$\nwhile\n$$\\Pi\\!\\left\\{\\theta:\\mathrm{KL}(P_0\\Vert P_\\theta)\\le\\epsilon_n^2,\\quad \\int\\left[\\log\\frac{p_\\theta}{p_0}\\right]^2p_0\\,d\\mu\\le\\epsilon_n^2\\right\\}\\ge e^{-Cn\\epsilon_n^2}.$$\nHere $\\mathrm{KL}(P_0\\Vert P_\\theta)$ has the directional convention of [[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]]. Define the data-indexed posterior by normalizing $\\prod_i p_\\theta(X_i)$ against $\\Pi$ on fibers with finite positive evidence. Then for all sufficiently large fixed $M$, the posterior contracts outside the $M\\epsilon_n$-ball in the selected metric:\n$$\\Pi\\{\\theta:d(p_\\theta,p_0)>M\\epsilon_n\\mid X_1,\\ldots,X_n\\}\\longrightarrow0$$\nin $P_0^n$-probability.\n\nFor $d=d_1$, the radius is in the full density L1 norm; by [[lem-hellinger-distance-controls-l1-and-total-variation]], $d_1=2\\lVert P-Q\\rVert_{\\rm TV}$ under the library event-supremum convention. The theorem asserts only convergence in probability and gives no automatic almost-sure or minimax-optimality conclusion."
    ],
